---
import JapanServicePage from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import type { Article } from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import { createPageSeo } from "@lib/seo/seo-config";
import { createBreadcrumbs } from "@lib/breadcrumbs/breadcrumbs";
import { usd, countryOgImage } from "@data/countries/international";
import { JPY_PER_USD, jpy, both, ja, japanRegions, japanServiceSchema } from "@data/countries/japan";

const path = "/japan/android-ios-developer/";
const prefectureCount = japanRegions.reduce((n, r) => n + r.prefectures.length, 0);

// ---------- FAQ (visible list and FAQPage schema share this array; plain text only) ----------

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How much does it cost to build an app in Japan?",
    answer: `Japanese 2026 price guides put contract app development at roughly ¥1,000,000 to ¥2,000,000 per platform, so an app built natively for both iPhone and Android often costs about twice that. Larger apps are quoted from ¥3,000,000, and very small apps from around ¥500,000. Our Android and iOS app plan starts from ${both("Android")} for one cross-platform app on both stores, including an admin panel. The final quote depends on screens, integrations, payments and the back end, and every figure here is a starting price rather than a fixed quote.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Why is your app price so much lower than Japanese app companies?",
    answer: "Two reasons. First, we build one Flutter or React Native codebase that runs on iPhone and Android instead of two separate native apps, which removes a large part of the work. Second, our costs are lower: we are three engineers in India with no Tokyo office, no sales team and no layers of subcontracting between you and the developers. You give something up for that price: everything happens in English and online, and your side has to write or check the Japanese text in the app.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are you based in Japan?",
    answer: "No. We are three engineers in India, and every project with a Japanese business is handled remotely. We have no office, business registration, staff or address in Japan, and nobody on the team speaks business Japanese. You talk directly with Ankur, Santosh and Vedansh, the people who plan the architecture, write the code and publish the app. If you need a vendor that runs meetings in Japanese or visits your premises, a Japanese company will suit you better, and we would rather say so before you sign anything.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you build with Flutter or React Native?",
    answer: "Both, and we choose per project. Flutter draws its own interface, so screens look the same on every phone and brand-heavy custom designs are straightforward. React Native uses native components and suits teams that already work in TypeScript or run a React website, because some logic can be shared. For most business apps in Japan either works well. We explain the choice in the scope document, and we recommend fully native Swift and Kotlin only when an app depends heavily on specialised device features.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you publish the app on the App Store and Google Play under our company name?",
    answer: "Yes, and it should always be your company's name. You enrol in the Apple Developer Program and open a Google Play developer account as an organisation, then invite us as team members. We prepare builds, screenshots, privacy details and store listing fields, submit them for review and answer reviewer questions. Store fees are paid by you directly to Apple and Google. Because the accounts are yours, the listings, ratings and download history stay with your company if you ever change developer.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can the app take payments with PayPay, JCB or konbini?",
    answer: "For physical goods and real-world services, such as products, takeout orders, salon bookings or deliveries, yes. The app can take cards including JCB through a payment provider, and konbini payment or PayPay through providers that support them, with each merchant account applied for, and reviewed by the provider, under your company. Digital content used inside the app, such as premium features or subscriptions, has generally fallen under Apple and Google in-app purchase rules, although Japan's smartphone competition law has opened alternatives. We check the current store terms for your case before designing checkout.",
  },
  {
    question: "What changed for apps under Japan's Mobile Software Competition Act?",
    answer: "The Mobile Software Competition Act designates Apple and Google as providers of smartphone operating systems and app stores, and its main obligations took full effect on 18 December 2025. It is intended to allow alternative app stores and alternative payment options for apps distributed in Japan, and both companies have published Japan-specific terms, fees and conditions for using them. The details are complex and still being refined, so we read the current terms for your app type before recommending a payment or distribution approach. This is not legal advice.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does it take to build an Android and iOS app?",
    answer: "A focused first version, such as a member app with login, a digital point card, news and push notifications, usually takes six to ten weeks from approved scope to store submission. Apps with bookings, payments, offline sync or several integrations take longer, and the quote includes a week-by-week plan. Store review adds time: Apple and Google often review within days, but a first submission can bring questions. Japanese text, approvals from several people and legal wording are the most common causes of delay.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you work in Japanese?",
    answer: "English is our working language. Nobody on the team can produce Japanese that reads naturally, so your app's Japanese wording never comes from us, and machine translation is never shipped as final copy. We do build the Japanese interface properly, with Japanese fonts and glyph shapes, sensible line breaks, name and furigana fields, postal code lookup, kana-to-kanji input handling and correct date formats, and every string lives in one translation file. Your staff, a bilingual colleague or a translator writes or reviews the Japanese, and we import it without copy-paste errors.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you add LINE Login and LINE messages to our app?",
    answer: "Yes. LINE Login lets customers sign in with the LINE account they already use, which removes a password step for many Japanese users, and the Messaging API can send booking reminders, order updates or point balances through your LINE Official Account. We link each LINE user to the member record on your server, respect opt-outs and keep message volume sensible, because LINE plans include a set number of messages. On iPhone, apps that offer third-party login may also need an additional privacy-focused login option under Apple's rules.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should we build a store app or a progressive web app?",
    answer: "If customers use you once or twice a year, a progressive web app or a fast mobile website is usually enough: no store review, no install and updates that go live instantly. A store app earns its cost when people return often, when you need dependable push notifications, a digital point card on the home screen, camera or Bluetooth features, or offline use for staff. A LINE-based mini app is a third option for shops whose customers already follow them on LINE. We recommend the cheapest option that meets the goal.",
  },
  {
    question: "Who owns the app source code?",
    answer: "You do. The source code sits in a Git repository owned by your company, the store listings are under your developer accounts, and the cloud, database, push notification and analytics accounts are opened in your name, with every credential handed over at launch. Once the project is paid, the code written for you is yours to keep, change or give to another developer. We also document how to build, sign and release the app, so a different team could take over without needing us.",
  },
  {
    question: "What does app maintenance cost after launch?",
    answer: `The first five months after release cost nothing and cover bug fixes, small changes, dependency and security updates, and help with store review questions on updates. From the sixth month, an ongoing plan starts from ${both("Maintenance")}. Apps need more upkeep than websites because Apple and Google release new operating system versions every year and periodically raise the SDK and API levels that updates must target. Servers, databases, push notifications and other outside services bill your company directly.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Where will the app back end and user data be hosted?",
    answer: "For apps whose users are mainly in Japan, we normally run the API and database in Tokyo on AWS or Google Cloud and copy backups to Osaka, so one regional outage cannot take everything down. Keeping data in Japan helps speed and keeps discussions with your advisers simple, although it does not change the APPI rules on who may access the data. Japanese price guides often quote around ¥20,000 a month for app servers; small apps can run on less, and the cloud provider bills your company directly.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can our app comply with the APPI?",
    answer: "The app is designed around the Act on the Protection of Personal Information: a stated purpose for each piece of data in the app and privacy policy, only the data each feature needs, encrypted connections, role-based admin access and account deletion on request. If our team in India can access personal data, that is provision to a third party in a foreign country under the APPI, which needs consent with information about India's system or an equivalent protection system. We minimise our access and test with dummy data. Your adviser should confirm the approach.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you charge Japanese consumption tax or issue qualified invoices?",
    answer: "No. We have not registered as a qualified invoice issuer in Japan, which means our invoices contain no Japanese consumption tax and cannot be used as qualified invoices for input tax credits. The consumption tax treatment of services bought from an overseas developer, and any withholding question, depends on your company's situation, so please ask your tax accountant (zeirishi). Japanese IT adoption subsidies also require registered support providers, and we are not one, so an app built by us cannot be funded that way.",
  },
  {
    question: "What hours are you available in Japan time?",
    answer: "We are reachable on WhatsApp every day from 9 am to 9 pm India time, or from 12:30 pm until 12:30 am JST. India is 3.5 hours behind Japan and no daylight saving applies in either country, so the gap stays the same all year. Afternoon calls are easy to schedule, test builds based on your morning feedback usually arrive the same evening, and for release days or urgent store review problems we can book an earlier slot, around 10 am JST, ahead of time.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do we pay, and can you invoice in yen?",
    answer: `Prices are quoted in US dollars, with approximate yen at ¥${JPY_PER_USD} per dollar. If your accounts team prefers yen, the quote states a JPY amount and that is what the invoice shows, whatever the currency markets do. Transfers go through Wise or an international bank wire, split into three stages: a deposit when work begins, a second instalment once the designs are signed off, and the remainder just before release. Your Apple and Google developer fees, cloud bills, LINE plan and payment processing charges go straight from your company to each provider.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can you add AI features to an app?",
    answer: `Yes, where they save real effort. Examples include a chat assistant that answers questions from your own FAQs and manuals, photo recognition that helps field staff log equipment, AI-OCR that reads receipts or paper orders, and summaries of customer feedback in the admin panel. AI calls run through your back end rather than the app, so keys stay private and usage can be capped. AI automation starts from ${both("AI automation")}, and model API usage is billed by the provider directly to you.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can you take over or rebuild an existing app?",
    answer: "Often, yes. We first ask for access to the source code, store accounts and back end, then check the framework version, dependencies, build setup and crash reports. Sometimes an update is enough; sometimes an app built on outdated tools is cheaper to rebuild in Flutter or React Native than to repair. Either way you get a written assessment before any work is quoted. If a previous developer still controls your store accounts or signing keys, recovering them comes first.",
  },
  {
    question: "What do you need from us to start an app project?",
    answer: "Just a short English message by WhatsApp or email covering what the app should do, who will use it, which platforms matter and which systems it must connect to, such as a website, kintone, Shopify or a point-of-sale system. Screenshots of apps you like help a lot. Our reply comes with questions first; after that you receive a written scope, a screen list, a schedule and a USD or JPY quote, normally within two working days. Nothing starts until you approve that scope.",
  },
];

// ---------- Page meta ----------

const page = createPageSeo({
