    heading: "Payments in apps: store rules, Japan's smartphone act, PayPay and konbini",
    paragraphs: [
      "Payment design is where app projects in Japan most often need a careful decision, because the store rules depend on what is being sold. Under Apple's App Review Guidelines and Google Play's payments policy, digital goods and features used inside the app, such as premium content, subscriptions to app features or virtual currency, have generally had to use the store's in-app purchase system, with the store taking a commission. Physical goods and real-world services, such as products shipped to a home, takeout orders, salon appointments or event tickets, are paid through ordinary payment methods instead.",
      `<strong>The Mobile Software Competition Act.</strong> Japan's ${ja("スマホソフトウェア競争促進法")} (Mobile Software Competition Act) designates Apple and Google as providers of smartphone operating systems and app stores, and it took full effect on 18 December 2025. It is intended to open the door to alternative app stores and alternative payment options for apps distributed in Japan, and both companies have published Japan-specific programmes with their own conditions and fees. The practical picture for a given app, including which commission applies when a user pays outside the store, depends on detailed terms that are still being refined. We read the current terms for your app type before recommending an approach, and your adviser should review anything contractual. This is not legal advice.`,
      "<strong>Physical goods and services.</strong> For shopping, delivery, bookings and B2B orders, we connect a payment provider that supports Japanese methods. Cards must include JCB alongside Visa, Mastercard and American Express. For Japanese businesses, Stripe handles cards, konbini and bank transfer, while local providers add options such as code payments and deferred payment. With konbini payment, the app shows a payment number and deadline, and the order is confirmed only when the payment notification arrives, so the order flow has to handle a pending state for days rather than seconds.",
