      "LINE has more than 100 million monthly users in Japan, according to LY Corporation's figures for the end of 2025, and for many customers it is the default way to hear from a business. Apps for Japan therefore often combine three channels: push notifications from the app itself, messages through a LINE Official Account, and email for receipts and account matters.",
      "<strong>LINE Login.</strong> Signing in with LINE removes a password step and gives you a LINE user ID to link to the member record, which in turn lets you send LINE messages to the right person. We store that link on your server, request only the profile permissions the feature needs and let users unlink. On iPhone, Apple's guidelines generally require an app that offers third-party login to offer an equivalent privacy-focused option as well, and Sign in with Apple is the usual way to meet that.",
      "<strong>LINE messaging.</strong> The Messaging API can send booking reminders, shipping updates, point balances and coupons, and can receive customer messages for a support flow. LINE Notify was shut down in March 2025, so staff alerts that relied on it have to be rebuilt on the Messaging API or moved to Slack, Chatwork or Microsoft Teams. LINE Official Account plans include a set number of messages, so we design flows that send fewer, more useful messages.",
      "<strong>Push notifications.</strong> iPhone and recent Android versions both ask users for permission before showing notifications, and a refused permission is hard to win back. We ask at a moment when the benefit is obvious, such as right after a booking, rather than at first launch, and we split notifications into categories users can switch on or off: bookings, points, campaigns and news. Push goes through Apple Push Notification service and Firebase Cloud Messaging, with the sending logic on your server so staff can schedule messages from the admin panel.",
