    list: [
      "<strong>Fonts and glyphs.</strong> iPhone uses Hiragino Sans for Japanese and Android uses Noto Sans CJK, so system fonts usually look right and add nothing to the download size. Custom Japanese fonts contain thousands of glyphs, so we bundle or subset them only when the brand needs it, and we set the Japanese locale so kanji render in their Japanese forms.",
      `<strong>Line breaks and text length.</strong> Japanese has no spaces between words, so buttons, headings and notifications can break in awkward places. We apply line-breaking rules (${ja("禁則処理")}) so small kana and punctuation never start a line, and we check every screen with the final Japanese text, which often differs greatly in length from the English draft.`,
      `<strong>Names.</strong> Family name first, in separate fields, with a reading field in katakana or hiragana (${ja("フリガナ")}) so staff can pronounce and sort names. Sorting in the admin panel follows the reading, not the kanji.`,
      "<strong>Addresses and postal codes.</strong> A seven-digit postal code fills in the prefecture, city and district from Japan Post's postal code data, and the address continues down to block, building and room number. Delivery apps also keep a separate field for the building name, which couriers rely on.",
      "<strong>Japanese input.</strong> Japanese keyboards type kana and then convert to kanji, and the return key often confirms a conversion rather than submitting. Forms must not validate or submit while text is still being composed, and search boxes should wait for the converted text. Full-width and half-width numbers, letters and katakana are normalised instead of rejected.",
      `<strong>Dates, times and money.</strong> Dates run year, month, day, often with the day of the week in brackets, and some audiences expect the Japanese era year (Reiwa) as well. Booking screens usually use a 24-hour clock. Consumer prices are shown including consumption tax, with a ${ja("税込")} (tax included) label where it helps.`,
