      "<strong>How we build offline mode.</strong> The app stores data in a local database on the phone, writes every change to a queue and syncs with the server when the connection returns. Photos are compressed and uploaded in the background. Each record shows a clear status, such as saved on phone, sending or synced, so staff know what the office can see. Where two people can edit the same record, we agree conflict rules in advance, for example the newest change wins for notes but a supervisor's approval can never be overwritten.",
      "<strong>Typical features.</strong> Checklists that follow your existing paper forms, photo capture with automatic timestamps, signatures on screen, barcode and QR scanning for equipment and parts, including JAN codes on products, GPS location where staff agree to share it, and PDF reports generated on the server in the layout your customers expect. Many Japanese companies still file stamped paper reports, so the PDF output often matters as much as the app.",
      `<strong>B2B ordering.</strong> Restaurant owners, shop managers and contractors order from their own price list, repeat last week's order with one tap, see delivery cut-off times and get confirmation instantly. The supplier's staff see orders in the admin panel or in kintone instead of retyping FAX sheets. Where FAX orders continue, the <a href="/japan/ai-automation-engineer/">AI automation engineer in Japan</a> page explains how AI-OCR can read them into the same system.`,
